.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# Legacy locations, all four of them. Everything the app generates lives under
# ~/.realmspinner now, and a checkout carrying these has them moved there on the next
# start (realmspinner/migrate.py) -- but the entries stay, because they are here
# until that has run and because REALMSPINNER_HOME can still point back at the
# checkout deliberately.
#
# Root-anchored, as /bench/ and /vendor/ are: unanchored, any future package
# directory with one of these names (say src/realmspinner/studio/assets/) would
# silently vanish from the index.
/assets/
# Benchmark runs: generated artifacts, copied rather than referenced, and
# reclaimed with `python -m realmspinner.bench prune`. The suites and recipes that
# define a run live in src/ and are tracked.
/bench/
/models/
/palettes/
/vendor/
# Copies of assets/jobs.sqlite taken before a campaign submit. The job database
# is under /assets/ and therefore untracked; a backup of it is no more tracked
# than the original, and a sweep campaign takes one per run.
/backups/
# Intermediate objects from native/build.ps1. The DLL it produces lands in
# /vendor/ with the other native binaries; this is just the scratch beside it.
/native/obj/
/scratch/
# `realmspinner sweep` output: measurement GLBs and logs, regenerable and large.
/sweep/
# Synergy MCP's code-knowledge-graph index for this repo: regenerable from a
# scan, rebuilt continuously by its own file watcher, and machine-local.
/.synergy/
.idea/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
dist/
/build/
*.egg-info/
# Claude Code's per-machine state stays out, but the skills are project
# knowledge (/exercise-mode, /realmspinner-audit) and travel with the tree. Git
# cannot re-include a file under an excluded directory, so the parent is
# matched with a glob and the one subdirectory negated.
.claude/*
!.claude/skills/
# imgui's window layout, rewritten every time the app is closed. Tracking it
# meant every run produced a diff and every branch produced a conflict, over a
# file that is per-machine by definition.
imgui.ini
# Screenshot-harness output (`dev/scripts/screenshot_modes.py --out`), and the
# `REALMSPINNER_HOME` each run is pointed at. Per-run, regenerable, photographed for
# one review -- never an input to anything, and tens of MB of PNGs a pass.
#
# **One pattern, not one line per review.** This had grown a fourth entry
# (`.tmp-ui-audit`, `.tmp-plotter-review*`, `.tmp-studio-audit`, and the Inker
# UX pass) because each reviewer picked a new directory name, and the line
# after the one they read did not cover them. A `git add -A` after a harness
# run commits whatever the newest name was, so the rule is the prefix.
/.tmp-*/

# Sample inputs, deliberately untracked as of 2026-08-24 and **not** merely for
# size. Three of these are third-party art this project has no right to
# redistribute: `light_world.png` and `zelda_1.jpg` are Nintendo-derived (a rip
# of A Link to the Past and a screenshot beside it, kept only as look
# references and colour-destroyed for that purpose), and the `*_base.obj`,
# `*_base.blend` and `*_spritesheet.png` files are ULPC-derived under
# CC-BY-SA/GPL with no attribution travelling beside them.
#
# All 20 were git-tracked, so making the repository public would have published
# them -- and hatchling's sdist default swept the whole ~26 MB into every
# `uv build`, which CI runs on every push. `pyproject.toml`'s
# `[tool.hatch.build.targets.sdist]` allowlist is the other half of this;
# `tests/test_release_hygiene.py` pins both.
#
# The history purge completed on 2026-09-03: the blobs were stripped from every
# earlier commit and the remote recreated, so nothing reachable carries them any
# more. `/examples/` stays untracked regardless, because the underlying assets
# are third-party and unlicensed for redistribution.
/examples/
# The same argument for a local character pack kept beside the docs (Quaternius's
# "Universal Base Characters", used to judge Troupe clips on a real mesh): its
# own licence file governs it, so it is never published from here.
/docs/examples/

# The agent-facing configuration -- the skills under `.claude/skills/` and the
# per-project settings beside them -- is tooling for whoever is driving this
# checkout, not part of the application or its documentation, so it is kept out
# of the published repository (2026-09-11). It stays on disk and keeps working
# locally; `git rm --cached` removed it from the index without touching the
# files. Nothing in the suite reads it: proven by deleting it in a throwaway
# worktree and running the whole suite there, which is the only honest check,
# because a `--cached` removal leaves the files present locally and would keep
# a local run green while CI broke on a clean checkout. Unlike `/examples/`
# above, no history purge was done -- the files are still readable in every
# earlier commit, and were never secret.
/.claude/

# Unsloth writes its torch.compile cache into the current directory when a training
# script runs from the repo root (dev/training/clay-assistant/train/*.py under Realmspinner's
# Python); machine-local, regenerable, and a ruff failure the moment it appears.
/unsloth_compiled_cache/

# Development material -- the plan file, the invariants ledger, dated
# measurements and their corpora, review screenshots, the Clay-assistant
# training programme, bench/calibration scripts and the tests that read them --
# lives under /dev/ and is kept out of the published repository (2026-09-16).
# It is maintainer-local working state, not part of the application or its
# documentation. Its tests are a local lane (`dev/tests`, listed in testpaths;
# pytest skips a testpath that does not exist on a clean checkout), and nothing
# in tests/ may read a file under dev/, or CI breaks on a clean clone. No
# history purge was done: these files remain readable in earlier commits.
/dev/
# Agent-facing configuration that only works at the repository root. CLAUDE.md
# is the development guide for whoever drives this checkout and cites /dev/
# throughout; .mcp.json hard-codes machine-local absolute paths.
/CLAUDE.md
/.mcp.json
